---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Button from "../ui/Button.astro";
import missionTeam from "../../assets/mission/mission-team.png";

/**
 * FULL REBUILD, not a restyle of an equivalent existing structure — read
 * this before assuming the six pieces below already existed in some
 * form. The PREVIOUS `MissionBand.astro` was a single centred pull-quote
 * on a solid Guard Green Deep fill (one italic `<h1>`-scale line + one
 * supporting sentence, both `text-paper`, no image, no caption, no
 * button, no two-column layout). None of the six pieces the brief
 * described as "existing structure, just restyle" — the "OUR MISSION"
 * caption, the "Meeting your needs, to excellence" headline, the
 * underline rule, a two-column image+text grid, and a "More about us"
 * button — were actually present anywhere in this codebase or in this
 * project's own external reference notes before this pass (confirmed
 * via a full repo grep and a `git log -S` search for each exact
 * phrase before writing anything, not assumed). Flagged plainly at the
 * time rather than silently building net-new content under a "restyle"
 * label; the two body paragraphs below ARE the previous version's own
 * two sentences, carried over as closely as the new layout allows (see
 * CONTENT note below) — that's the one piece of continuity that's real.
 *
 * BACKGROUND — plain Paper (white), no colour fill at all. A deliberate
 * pacing choice, not an oversight: Hero/StatStrip/Services are all
 * coloured (Electric Blue/Magenta/Amber), so this section is the "pause"
 * on white before Sectors/Closing CTA. Guard Green Deep — this
 * section's own fill since the very first build — is retired from here
 * as a result. See the RETIREMENT note at the bottom of this comment for
 * the full "is the token still needed anywhere" accounting; short
 * version: yes, one dormant reference elsewhere, not deleted.
 *
 * CONTENT, kept per explicit instruction not to simplify or remove
 * anything the brief listed — six pieces, in DOM/reading order: "OUR
 * MISSION" caption → headline → underline rule → two body paragraphs →
 * "More about us" button.
 *
 * "OUR MISSION" — REAL, DIRECT HARD-RULE OVERRIDE, not an oversight.
 * CLAUDE.md's own hard rules ban "tracked-out ALL-CAPS eyebrow labels
 * above headings," naming "OUR MISSION" as the literal example of what's
 * banned. The brief asked for exactly that label, verbatim, by explicit
 * direct instruction — per that same hard-rules section's own stated
 * exception clause ("do not violate these... unless the user explicitly
 * overrides one"), this qualifies. CLAUDE.md's rule text is updated in
 * this same commit (a second, scoped, MissionBand-only exception,
 * mirroring how the ServicesGrid card-grid and Amber/Electric-Blue
 * exceptions were both handled — build the exception, then keep the
 * doc and the code in agreement) — don't read the rule in isolation and
 * assume this is a mistake sitting on top of it.
 *
 * The two BODY PARAGRAPHS are the previous version's own two sentences,
 * carried over — with one small, disclosed edit: the first sentence was
 * previously wrapped in literal curly-quote characters as a pull-quote
 * ("Give clients complete peace of mind…"); those quote marks are typo-
 * graphic punctuation belonging to the OLD centred-pull-quote framing,
 * not part of the sentence itself, and read oddly on a plain declarative
 * paragraph in a two-column layout — stripped here, the sentence itself
 * is otherwise byte-for-byte unchanged. The second sentence is carried
 * over verbatim, no edit at all.
 *
 * IMAGE — new asset, `src/assets/mission/mission-team.png` (1536×1024,
 * confirmed via `sharp` metadata, not guessed), following the exact
 * placement convention `hero-officer.png` already established
 * (`src/assets/<section>/<name>.png`, imported and rendered through
 * `astro:assets`' `<Image />` for responsive webp generation — never a
 * plain `<img src={...}>`). AI-generated (not a photograph of real House
 * of Guards staff) — noted here for provenance/transparency only, not
 * as a "placeholder, ask before final" flag: this is the real, final
 * asset for this section per direct instruction, the same status
 * `hero-officer.png` already has, not one of the still-placeholder
 * Picsum photos `SectorsStrip.astro` flags with that exact caveat.
 *
 * REAL GEOMETRY, checked not guessed (same discipline `Hero.astro`'s own
 * comment already established for its own photo) — the four officers
 * stand shoulder-to-shoulder across nearly the full frame width: cropped
 * left/right edge strips (0–220px and 1316–1536px of the 1536px-wide
 * source) confirmed the leftmost officer's body starts at roughly
 * x≈70–90px (≈5–6%) and the rightmost officer's arm extends to roughly
 * x≈1446–1466px (≈95%), leaving only ~5% of true margin on EITHER side.
 * This rules out the tall/narrow portrait crop a two-column image
 * column might otherwise default to — even a modest symmetric crop
 * (e.g. toward a 4:3 box) would already cut into an officer's arm on
 * both edges. The image is therefore rendered at its own real native
 * ratio, `aspect-[3/2]` (1536:1024 = 1.5 exactly), uncropped — object-
 * cover at this exact matching ratio performs no crop at all, it's
 * there purely as a robustness net against any future sub-pixel
 * rounding, not because cropping is actually happening. A landscape
 * image column is therefore a genuine, checked constraint of the source
 * photo, not a layout choice fought against it.
 *
 * LAYOUT — the proven two-column grid, kept exactly as asked
 * (`grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2`, image first in DOM/first column, text
 * second — stacks to a single column on mobile with the image on top,
 * matching the DOM order rather than introducing a reordering utility
 * for a stacking direction the brief never specified). `md:items-center`
 * vertically balances the two columns against each other. The "premium
 * upgrade" is deliberately NOT a new layout — see MOTION below for
 * where that actually comes from.
 *
 * ALIGNMENT — LEFT-aligned within the text column, at every breakpoint,
 * including mobile. This is a REAL, DELIBERATE change to a standing hard
 * rule, not an accidental drift the brief asked to be caught: CLAUDE.md
 * previously named this section as one of exactly two sanctioned
 * centred-alignment exceptions sitewide (the old pull-quote WAS centred,
 * `text-center` on its wrapper). That's gone now that this is a routine
 * left-aligned text column next to an image, the same register every
 * other section on the page uses — CLAUDE.md's hard rule is updated in
 * this same commit to drop MissionBand from that list (down to exactly
 * one exception, ClosingCta), matching this file's own established
 * "when a rule changes, update the rule text in the same commit" habit
 * (see the rule's own history note about StatStrip's brief third
 * exception for the precedent this follows).
 *
 * COLOUR — three real pairings, one of them a genuine, disclosed
 * concern worth reading in full, not glossed over:
 *   - Body text: Ink on Paper, ~19.44:1 (AAA) — the sitewide default,
 *     no new computation needed, restated here only for completeness
 *     with every other pairing on this page.
 *   - Button: `<Button href="/about">More about us</Button>` — the
 *     UNMODIFIED `primary` variant (Amber fill, Ink text, ~9.05:1 AAA),
 *     the exact same component every other primary CTA on the site
 *     uses, not a one-off style. See `Button.astro`'s own comment for
 *     the full derivation.
 *   - Caption + underline rule: Amber. The underline (a decorative,
 *     non-text 2px bar) reuses the ALREADY-REVIEWED-AND-ACCEPTED
 *     bare-mark-on-Paper pairing (`NavLink.astro`'s own active
 *     underline, ~2.15:1, below the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 floor, explicitly
 *     confirmed as a kept tradeoff in PR #18) — consistent precedent,
 *     not a new problem. **The CAPTION TEXT is a genuinely NEW and
 *     MORE SEVERE issue, flagged here plainly rather than assumed
 *     equivalent to the underline**: this is the SAME two colours
 *     (Amber vs Paper, ~2.15:1 — contrast is a pure function of the two
 *     colours, unaffected by what renders them), but text needs the
 *     4.5:1 normal-text floor (or 3:1 at large-text sizes, which
 *     `text-caption` at 14px/500 doesn't qualify for, checked the same
 *     way ServicesGrid's own body-text pass already checked this exact
 *     threshold question — nowhere close either way), a materially
 *     STRICTER bar than the 3:1 non-text floor the underline only has
 *     to clear. ~2.15:1 fails BOTH floors, and unlike the underline,
 *     no prior review has ever accepted Amber as an actual TEXT colour
 *     anywhere on this site — every existing Amber use is either a
 *     button fill (Ink text sits ON TOP of it) or a bare decorative
 *     mark. Shipped per the explicit "Caption label… Amber" instruction
 *     — not silently treated as fine. Flagged prominently in the PR
 *     description for a real decision, the same way Hero's thin scrim
 *     margin and NavLink's own underline were both flagged and then
 *     explicitly resolved by the user in PR #17/#18, rather than
 *     assumed acceptable by default.
 *
 * MOTION — this section's real "premium upgrade." See
 * `src/lib/missionReveal.ts`'s own comment for the full mechanism
 * (image: pure clip-path vertical unveil, no opacity change; text: a
 * five-beat staggered fade+rise on one shared GSAP `stagger`, same
 * mechanism `heroMuster.ts` already uses). The two body paragraphs
 * share ONE `[data-mission-reveal]` wrapper (one reveal beat, not two)
 * — the brief's own reading-order list names "paragraphs" once, plural,
 * as a single step between the underline and the button, not "paragraph
 * one, then paragraph two" as two separate steps, so this reads as
 * five sequential beats (caption, headline, rule, paragraphs together,
 * button), matching that list exactly rather than inventing a sixth.
 * New, dedicated module — not bolted onto `scrollReveal.ts`'s existing
 * fade/rise (this needed a genuinely different clip-path MECHANISM for
 * the image, which that module has no concept of) and not a copy of
 * `servicesGridReveal.ts`'s own per-card-independent-trigger shape
 * (this section has one shared trigger for its whole text column, not
 * six independent ones) — see that module's own comment for why one
 * shared GSAP `stagger` is the correct tool here, not a hand-rolled
 * per-element delay.
 *
 * RETIREMENT — Guard Green Deep is now retired from every homepage
 * section (Header/Hero/StatStrip/ServicesGrid were already retired in
 * the earlier rebrand; this section is the last one that still used it,
 * confirmed via grep after this edit that the bare, no-modifier Guard
 * Green Deep fill class no longer appears in ANY `.astro` component's
 * real rendered markup — this sentence deliberately doesn't spell that
 * exact class name out as one contiguous backtick-quoted token, since
 * it's now genuinely orphaned and this file's own comments are Tailwind-
 * scanned; see the dead-CSS-from-comments standing convention in
 * CLAUDE.md). The TOKEN ITSELF still has to stay defined in
 * `global.css` — it is NOT
 * safe to delete: `Button.astro`'s dormant `secondary` variant still
 * has a real, literal `hover:bg-guard-green-deep` class string in its
 * own source. That variant has zero call sites anywhere on the site (so
 * nothing actually RENDERS this colour today), but the class name still
 * exists in scanned source, which is all Tailwind's build-time scanner
 * cares about — deleting the theme token would silently break that
 * dormant variant's compiled CSS the moment it's ever activated. See
 * CLAUDE.md's own updated SCOPE note for the full, current accounting
 * of both Guard Green tokens now that this section no longer uses
 * either one.
 */
---

<section class="bg-paper">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-gutter py-24 md:px-12 md:py-32">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 items-center gap-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-16 lg:gap-20">
      <div class="aspect-[3/2] overflow-hidden">
        <Image
          src={missionTeam}
          alt="Four House of Guards officers standing together on a city street, backs to camera, wearing branded security jackets"
          class="h-full w-full object-cover"
          data-mission-image
          widths={[400, 600, 768, 1024, 1536]}
          sizes="(min-width: 768px) 50vw, 100vw"
          format="webp"
          quality={82}
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>

      <div class="text-left">
        <p data-mission-reveal class="text-caption text-amber uppercase tracking-[0.14em]">Our mission</p>

        <h2 data-mission-reveal class="text-h2 text-ink mt-4">Meeting your needs, to excellence</h2>

        <span data-mission-reveal aria-hidden="true" class="mt-5 block h-0.5 w-12 bg-amber"></span>

        <div data-mission-reveal class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          <p class="text-body text-ink max-w-md">
            Give clients complete peace of mind, while we keep watch.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body text-ink max-w-md">
            Our aim is to become a leading supplier of close protection and
            guarding services across the North West and Yorkshire.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div data-mission-reveal class="mt-8">
          <Button href="/about">More about us</Button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
